WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:03.803
Just when the weather is glorious,

00:00:04.463 --> 00:00:05.144
they come up.

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:11.570
They attack us and ruin our day.

00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:17.114
So every summer we get out our swatters,

00:00:17.594 --> 00:00:18.215
sprays,

00:00:18.535 --> 00:00:21.978
and repellents until they drive us indoors.

00:00:24.561 --> 00:00:26.201
It's us against them.

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:45.230
They emerge by the millions.

00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:51.573
They can navigate at night by moon and stars.

00:00:54.555 --> 00:00:56.796
They can walk upside down on a flower.

00:00:59.427 --> 00:01:01.949
From the top of the world almost to the bottom,

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.213
they bombard us on every continent except Antarctica.

00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:12.200
Mosquitoes are one of the most studied creatures on the planet.

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:17.503
It's the female mosquito that matters.

00:01:18.704 --> 00:01:24.790
It's the fact that females are the ones who are driving these systems that I found so interesting.

00:01:27.707 --> 00:01:35.015
It's the female who just has this immense drive to reproduce.

00:01:36.036 --> 00:01:40.159
Fiona Hunter is a medical entomologist at Brock University.

00:01:43.475 --> 00:01:48.599
It's the female who has to get a blood meal in order to mature a batch of eggs.

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.201
It's all about her passing on her genes to the next generation.

00:01:53.082 --> 00:01:55.463
Working with mosquitoes is a fiddly business.

00:01:55.963 --> 00:01:56.984
But at Hunter's lab,

00:01:57.384 --> 00:01:58.286
the female mosquitoes...

00:01:58.786 --> 00:01:59.765
still charms her.

00:02:01.646 --> 00:02:03.686
I think female mosquitoes are fantastic.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:06.427
They are beautiful.

00:02:07.188 --> 00:02:08.769
They're so versatile that they,

00:02:08.968 --> 00:02:09.929
against all odds,

00:02:09.968 --> 00:02:10.449
survive.

00:02:11.609 --> 00:02:12.129
They're amazing.

00:02:15.349 --> 00:02:17.390
But she's hell-bent on biting us,

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.551
leaving us fearing the diseases she transmits.

00:02:25.383 --> 00:02:37.770
She's a bloodsucker and by doing that she's actually able to pass pathogens from animals to humans or from animals to animals or even humans to humans.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:45.533
So she's the one who's the driving force of all the anguish that we feel when we hear mosquitoes buzzing.

00:02:47.014 --> 00:02:48.955
This summer pest needs us.

00:02:51.175 --> 00:02:52.317
She's attuned to us.

00:02:52.937 --> 00:02:54.259
She senses our movement.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.483
While we're out there enjoying the warm weather,

00:02:58.943 --> 00:02:59.764
she attacks.

00:03:01.025 --> 00:03:03.766
Her compound eyes keep us in her field of vision.

00:03:05.549 --> 00:03:07.270
But we're only silhouettes to her.

00:03:08.651 --> 00:03:11.093
Our body odor is the big giveaway.

00:03:11.753 --> 00:03:13.096
The chemicals we give off,

00:03:13.175 --> 00:03:15.418
like carbon dioxide and lactic acid,

00:03:15.937 --> 00:03:16.558
entice her.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:20.240
she uses her antennae to smell us.

00:03:22.822 --> 00:03:24.883
They may be less bushy than the males,

00:03:25.384 --> 00:03:26.464
but they do their job,

00:03:26.925 --> 00:03:28.506
wafting our scents toward her.

00:03:32.668 --> 00:03:33.729
The more we swat,

00:03:34.009 --> 00:03:37.291
the sweatier we get and the more chemicals we release.

00:03:38.112 --> 00:03:40.813
And the easier it is for her to find us.

00:03:42.074 --> 00:03:43.015
And take a bite.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:53.272
She alights on our skin and probes ever so gently for the perfect spot to jab with her tiny proboscis.

00:03:54.713 --> 00:03:55.933
It's a loaded barrel.

00:03:57.413 --> 00:03:58.034
Inside,

00:03:58.055 --> 00:04:01.635
there's a spear for piercing our skin and two tubes.

00:04:03.115 --> 00:04:03.756
Through one,

00:04:04.316 --> 00:04:07.796
she administers an anesthetic and an anticoagulant.

00:04:09.978 --> 00:04:10.677
Through the other,

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:12.258
she sucks blood.

00:04:17.478 --> 00:04:21.501
The iron and protein in it are what she needs to develop her eggs.

00:04:23.142 --> 00:04:23.622
But now,

00:04:24.002 --> 00:04:25.283
she's too heavy to fly.

00:04:25.883 --> 00:04:26.444
So first,

00:04:26.604 --> 00:04:29.165
she digests her meal and eliminates water.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:36.390
Then,

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:37.470
sniffing around,

00:04:37.911 --> 00:04:40.072
she searches out a place to lay her eggs,

00:04:40.492 --> 00:04:42.213
where others have already been laid.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.252
This is prime mosquito habitat.

00:04:48.592 --> 00:04:50.092
It's nice and damp.

00:04:50.473 --> 00:04:55.355
The adult females can actually rest in the vegetation during the heat of the day,

00:04:56.735 --> 00:04:58.496
so they're not going to dry out.

00:04:59.837 --> 00:05:09.322
And the convenient thing is that we have standing water right next to this area where the females can be laying their eggs.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.132
She lays her eggs either singly or in a raft,

00:05:18.473 --> 00:05:20.814
and either in mud or pools of water.

00:05:23.636 --> 00:05:25.418
As she squeezes out her eggs,

00:05:25.878 --> 00:05:28.920
sperm stored in a special pouch fertilizes them.

00:05:29.761 --> 00:05:30.762
She'll do this again,

00:05:30.922 --> 00:05:32.403
feeding and laying eggs,

00:05:32.783 --> 00:05:35.145
until her life ends several days later.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:38.811
She's not much of a mother.

00:05:39.171 --> 00:05:44.234
All she does is lays her eggs and leaves them and who knows what's going to happen to them.

00:05:46.536 --> 00:05:49.697
Her offspring start their lives as tiny larvae.

00:05:52.738 --> 00:05:58.682
At this stage they're perfect underwater creatures that breathe through a siphon at the surface of the water.

00:06:01.563 --> 00:06:05.966
Their whiskers create water currents that drive food to their hungry mouths.

00:06:09.262 --> 00:06:11.243
But it's a violent world they live in.

00:06:14.766 --> 00:06:16.067
As they wriggle around,

00:06:16.507 --> 00:06:18.188
molting through four stages,

00:06:18.748 --> 00:06:20.391
only a fraction survive.

00:06:24.272 --> 00:06:27.094
The lucky ones turn into comma-shaped pupae,

00:06:27.675 --> 00:06:38.022
which tumble about until a fully-formed mosquito splits open its case and crawls out.

00:06:45.358 --> 00:06:50.622
She gets her insect blood pumping and almost immediately has liftoff.

00:06:51.444 --> 00:06:56.608
Slow motion shows that she beats her wings up to a thousand times per second.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:00.831
Within a day or two,

00:07:01.072 --> 00:07:02.052
she has matured.

00:07:05.534 --> 00:07:06.016
And now,

00:07:06.576 --> 00:07:08.937
she needs a mate to fertilize her eggs.

00:07:11.118 --> 00:07:13.620
There are lots of males waiting for her.

00:07:14.741 --> 00:07:17.644
Tracking software reveals what goes on.

00:07:19.185 --> 00:07:19.764
He's blue,

00:07:20.104 --> 00:07:20.765
she's red.

00:07:21.305 --> 00:07:24.168
She enters the swarm and flies around its lower half,

00:07:24.608 --> 00:07:25.949
faster than all the males,

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:28.831
until a lucky one catches up with her.

00:07:30.271 --> 00:07:31.252
How do they hook up?

00:07:32.313 --> 00:07:33.694
They sing to each other.

00:07:34.555 --> 00:07:37.716
Hold a male mosquito and a female up to a microphone,

00:07:38.337 --> 00:07:39.858
and this is what you'll hear.

00:07:50.742 --> 00:07:52.983
Now the flirting and foreplay begin.

00:07:58.125 --> 00:07:59.646
And then it happens.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:01.848
Here it is in real time.

00:08:02.367 --> 00:08:03.108
She's on top,

00:08:03.168 --> 00:08:03.728
of course.

00:08:05.329 --> 00:08:06.670
And that's it for the male.

00:08:07.209 --> 00:08:08.850
The end of his life is near.

00:08:10.499 --> 00:08:10.819
Really,

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:15.264
a male's only job is to sip on a little nectar,

00:08:15.463 --> 00:08:20.269
get some sugar in him so that he has enough energy to fly around and find a female,

00:08:20.908 --> 00:08:21.810
and then to mate.

00:08:22.778 --> 00:08:23.798
After ditching him,

00:08:24.319 --> 00:08:25.919
she puts the moves on us.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.420
But who to get intimate with?

00:08:29.421 --> 00:08:31.041
Is it you over others?

00:08:31.961 --> 00:08:32.922
Women over men?

00:08:34.282 --> 00:08:35.563
Blondes over brunettes?

00:08:37.263 --> 00:08:38.884
It's more complicated than that.

00:08:39.724 --> 00:08:43.226
She's after the one giving off the most enticing chemicals.

00:08:44.154 --> 00:08:44.354
You know,

00:08:44.355 --> 00:08:49.358
you have these insects that are flying through what you might imagine is a sort of aerial soup of chemicals,

00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:52.160
being given off by everything in the environment,

00:08:52.341 --> 00:08:59.586
yet they're so sophisticated that they know to ignore those chemicals and focus in on the ones that are coming from us or from their breeding site.

00:09:00.187 --> 00:09:00.387
And,

00:09:00.567 --> 00:09:00.807
you know,

00:09:00.808 --> 00:09:04.851
just understanding that sort of hidden world of communication is absolutely fascinating.

00:09:05.291 --> 00:09:07.533
James Logan is a medical entomologist.

00:09:09.582 --> 00:09:18.566
We're actually sort of down in the dungeons of the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine and down in these vaults we've got lots of different mosquito species.

00:09:19.887 --> 00:09:24.809
Logan collects these for his research on mosquitoes and our body odour.

00:09:26.206 --> 00:09:26.446
Yes,

00:09:26.626 --> 00:09:27.106
that's right.

00:09:27.226 --> 00:09:27.347
So

00:09:28.327 --> 00:09:29.587
I am the guru on smell.

00:09:30.848 --> 00:09:33.169
So I guess I call myself a chemical ecologist.

00:09:33.509 --> 00:09:38.891
And that means I'm interested in trying to find out how insects use their sense of smell to find us.

00:09:39.372 --> 00:09:39.672
Okay,

00:09:39.792 --> 00:09:44.174
so we're going to do an experiment to find out how attractive you are to mosquitoes.

00:09:44.874 --> 00:09:48.495
Logan uses this wind tunnel to come up with two groups of people,

00:09:49.115 --> 00:09:50.195
those who are most,

00:09:50.576 --> 00:09:51.596
and more importantly,

00:09:52.076 --> 00:09:53.876
least attractive to mosquitoes.

00:09:54.617 --> 00:09:55.637
To collect your body odor,

00:09:55.977 --> 00:09:57.697
we use this foil bag.

00:09:59.318 --> 00:10:01.999
There are 500 chemicals in our body odor.

00:10:02.619 --> 00:10:06.160
He wants to know exactly which ones attract mosquitoes.

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.060
I'm going to seal it just around your neck,

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:08.380
very,

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:08.720
very,

00:10:09.841 --> 00:10:10.441
very gently.

00:10:10.621 --> 00:10:11.261
So first,

00:10:11.521 --> 00:10:14.442
he has a volunteer slip into an inflatable bag.

00:10:16.663 --> 00:10:19.565
and if you just turn and put your feet on the...

00:10:20.685 --> 00:10:20.925
Then,

00:10:21.065 --> 00:10:22.286
with tubes connected,

00:10:22.666 --> 00:10:24.848
he sends purified air over her.

00:10:25.928 --> 00:10:28.950
This directs her body odors to a filter that traps them.

00:10:31.231 --> 00:10:32.632
It takes about two hours,

00:10:33.152 --> 00:10:34.673
plenty long enough to feel...

00:10:35.173 --> 00:10:35.813
Hot.

00:10:35.833 --> 00:10:36.814
Is it hot?

00:10:36.815 --> 00:10:36.934
Plenty.

00:10:39.976 --> 00:10:40.316
Next,

00:10:40.776 --> 00:10:43.278
Logan runs her chemicals past a mosquito.

00:10:44.974 --> 00:10:45.474
For that,

00:10:45.895 --> 00:10:48.177
some microscopic surgery is required.

00:10:48.898 --> 00:10:51.260
All he needs are the mosquito's antennae.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.562
So he severs her head.

00:10:54.563 --> 00:10:55.664
It's delicate work.

00:10:59.347 --> 00:11:00.248
I've kind of lost the head.

00:11:01.869 --> 00:11:02.870
He begins again,

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:09.176
and this time he manages to attach a pair of very fine glass electrodes to her antennae,

00:11:09.736 --> 00:11:11.558
which remain active for a while.

00:11:13.974 --> 00:11:15.215
That creates a circuit,

00:11:15.635 --> 00:11:18.597
and as he directs the body odors across the antennae,

00:11:19.157 --> 00:11:20.438
the mosquito reacts,

00:11:20.878 --> 00:11:22.539
creating electrical signals.

00:11:23.699 --> 00:11:24.060
So here,

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:24.700
for example,

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:28.602
you can see there's a nice big response from the antenna to this chemical here.

00:11:28.882 --> 00:11:33.645
His computer program matches the mosquito's responses to actual chemicals.

00:11:34.185 --> 00:11:34.745
And voila,

00:11:35.406 --> 00:11:36.326
they're identified.

00:11:38.347 --> 00:11:39.648
After hundreds of tests,

00:11:40.108 --> 00:11:42.750
Logan will know which chemicals in our body odor

00:11:43.178 --> 00:11:44.599
Mosquitoes don't like.

00:11:45.339 --> 00:11:45.920
From that,

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:48.021
he'll create a natural repellent.

00:11:49.441 --> 00:11:50.002
Ultimately,

00:11:50.482 --> 00:11:53.324
Logan's hope is to find a way to block the mosquitoes'

00:11:53.384 --> 00:11:56.685
receptors so they can't smell us in the first place.

00:11:58.786 --> 00:12:01.108
Every day we sort of get an inch further,

00:12:01.109 --> 00:12:05.830
an inch closer to understanding more about these insects that spread these devastating diseases.

00:12:07.991 --> 00:12:12.514
West Nile is one of the most serious diseases transmitted by mosquitoes.

00:12:13.162 --> 00:12:17.725
It hit Canada via Europe and the United States in 2002,

00:12:18.325 --> 00:12:20.686
and that's when Fiona Hunter got hooked.

00:12:21.887 --> 00:12:22.867
Mosquitoes were not,

00:12:22.947 --> 00:12:23.348
I admit,

00:12:23.408 --> 00:12:24.688
my first love.

00:12:25.409 --> 00:12:27.670
But then they got under her skin.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:32.032
I had a lab capable of identifying biting insects,

00:12:32.253 --> 00:12:36.675
so it was sort of thrust upon us that,

00:12:37.856 --> 00:12:39.777
and a sense of duty that

00:12:40.117 --> 00:12:41.678
I better jump in and...

00:12:42.102 --> 00:12:43.823
do something.

00:12:43.824 --> 00:12:48.025
Now Hunter is an authority on them and assisted by Ainsley Teelman,

00:12:48.365 --> 00:12:49.665
a research scientist,

00:12:50.025 --> 00:12:56.268
she runs a government-funded surveillance program that roots out dangerous mosquitoes and their viruses.

00:12:59.429 --> 00:13:00.410
Across Ontario,

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.011
teams set mosquito traps nightly.

00:13:06.693 --> 00:13:09.734
They use dry ice as bait to entice them

00:13:10.102 --> 00:13:13.767
with its delicious animal-like smell of carbon dioxide.

00:13:17.792 --> 00:13:18.413
It works.

00:13:20.655 --> 00:13:22.618
The traps are sent to Hunter's facility.

00:13:23.318 --> 00:13:27.581
The millions of mosquitoes that arrive daily are sorted by species.

00:13:29.583 --> 00:13:30.883
It's the dangerous ones

00:13:31.224 --> 00:13:32.685
Hunter's team is looking for.

00:13:33.485 --> 00:13:36.467
The ones that carry the West Nile virus or worse.

00:13:38.208 --> 00:13:39.329
It's meticulous work,

00:13:39.769 --> 00:13:40.410
but critical.

00:13:42.142 --> 00:13:48.726
I think that with climate changing and invasive species coming into Canada,

00:13:49.207 --> 00:13:51.768
we're going to see more mosquito-borne diseases.

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:55.410
It just takes one.

00:13:55.971 --> 00:13:56.971
But the question is,

00:13:57.432 --> 00:13:59.913
which kind of mosquito just bit us?

00:14:03.694 --> 00:14:05.695
The Natural History Museum in London,

00:14:05.875 --> 00:14:06.295
England,

00:14:06.735 --> 00:14:10.197
holds one of the largest collections of mosquitoes in the world.

00:14:10.877 --> 00:14:14.159
The museum's authority on mosquitoes is Ralph Harback.

00:14:14.679 --> 00:14:16.860
Ask him what's fascinating about them.

00:14:18.321 --> 00:14:20.342
The diversity of mosquitoes,

00:14:20.802 --> 00:14:23.083
not just the form of how they look.

00:14:23.163 --> 00:14:24.023
Some are beautiful,

00:14:24.143 --> 00:14:24.504
gorgeous,

00:14:24.544 --> 00:14:26.244
with metallic blues and greens.

00:14:27.545 --> 00:14:28.906
Others are very drab and ugly.

00:14:30.206 --> 00:14:33.210
In a cooled and sealed room in the museum's basement,

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:37.114
rows of cabinets store half a million mosquitoes.

00:14:39.757 --> 00:14:42.480
Most of them were found in countries of the British Empire.

00:14:44.322 --> 00:14:46.765
Each specimen is a valuable reference,

00:14:47.906 --> 00:14:50.750
like this ancient mosquito preserved in amber.

00:14:51.862 --> 00:14:55.125
The earliest fossil mosquito we have comes from the Cretaceous,

00:14:55.766 --> 00:14:58.008
which is somewhere around 100 million years old,

00:14:59.109 --> 00:15:01.171
toward the end of the reign of the dinosaurs.

00:15:02.032 --> 00:15:06.777
And we think that mosquitoes probably evolved even a bit earlier than that.

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:11.052
Some scientists theorize that mosquitoes,

00:15:11.333 --> 00:15:12.834
along with other insects,

00:15:13.274 --> 00:15:15.276
are linked to the death of dinosaurs.

00:15:15.957 --> 00:15:21.742
That's because traces of deadly diseases have been found inside ancient mosquito specimens.

00:15:22.603 --> 00:15:25.585
There's no speculation when it comes to humans.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:32.955
Mosquitoes are the indirect cause of more morbidity and mortality than any other organism on the planet.

00:15:33.055 --> 00:15:35.717
The key word is indirect,

00:15:36.137 --> 00:15:37.678
because they're not causing the disease.

00:15:37.679 --> 00:15:43.563
They're just transmitting the organisms into the human body that cause the disease.

00:15:45.084 --> 00:15:47.386
Not all mosquitoes transmit disease.

00:15:48.894 --> 00:15:52.816
Of the 3,523 species that we currently recognize,

00:15:53.257 --> 00:15:58.820
there are only perhaps 150 that are nuisance or transmit diseases,

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:00.661
perhaps 200 at most.

00:16:01.341 --> 00:16:03.923
Those 200 mosquito species belong,

00:16:04.263 --> 00:16:05.064
by and large,

00:16:05.304 --> 00:16:06.665
to just three groups,

00:16:07.185 --> 00:16:07.645
Culex,

00:16:08.105 --> 00:16:08.786
Anopheles,

00:16:09.126 --> 00:16:09.886
and Aedes.

00:16:10.707 --> 00:16:15.730
They're the most dangerous mosquitoes on the planet and the ones that need to be controlled.

00:16:17.562 --> 00:16:19.263
If you're going to control mosquitoes,

00:16:19.523 --> 00:16:19.903
kill them,

00:16:20.503 --> 00:16:24.004
so that they're not a nuisance or not there to transmit disease,

00:16:24.005 --> 00:16:27.805
you have to know something about their behavior and their ecology.

00:16:28.966 --> 00:16:30.126
First up is Culex.

00:16:30.787 --> 00:16:36.889
She's the common household mosquito around the world and the transmitter of the West Nile virus.

00:16:37.549 --> 00:16:40.910
That makes her the most dangerous mosquito in Canada.

00:16:44.982 --> 00:16:47.503
She likes to breed in polluted pools of water,

00:16:48.043 --> 00:16:51.144
laying hundreds of eggs in rafts every few days.

00:16:54.125 --> 00:16:56.406
Culex mosquitoes are bird biters.

00:17:02.488 --> 00:17:04.568
If the bird has the West Nile virus,

00:17:04.868 --> 00:17:05.629
she picks it up,

00:17:06.209 --> 00:17:08.169
and if a person is handy,

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:09.510
passes it on.

00:17:11.194 --> 00:17:16.037
I wouldn't think that an insect that is attracted to a bird would also be attracted to a human.

00:17:16.097 --> 00:17:16.938
We look different,

00:17:17.018 --> 00:17:18.419
we smell different,

00:17:18.499 --> 00:17:20.621
we live in different environments.

00:17:22.762 --> 00:17:24.663
But she does like our blood too,

00:17:25.284 --> 00:17:27.926
so she bites whichever of us is more available.

00:17:30.058 --> 00:17:30.378
Next,

00:17:30.658 --> 00:17:31.979
the Anopheles mosquito.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:38.063
She's the world's most dangerous mosquito because she transmits the malaria parasite.

00:17:38.803 --> 00:17:40.624
Anopheles live in Canada too,

00:17:41.325 --> 00:17:43.906
but with no parasite here for her to transmit,

00:17:44.366 --> 00:17:45.567
she's just a nuisance.

00:17:47.808 --> 00:17:49.409
She calls wetlands home.

00:17:51.402 --> 00:17:55.606
That's where Ainsley Teelman dips for larvae that she will rear in a lab.

00:17:57.408 --> 00:17:59.750
There's about five different species of Monopoly's in Canada,

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:00.851
and all of them,

00:18:00.891 --> 00:18:03.133
this is their most ideal habitat,

00:18:03.213 --> 00:18:05.435
ponds that have a lot of algae,

00:18:06.216 --> 00:18:08.638
because I think they like to rest on top of the algae.

00:18:09.579 --> 00:18:11.081
More than 200 years ago,

00:18:11.781 --> 00:18:16.305
these mosquitoes transmitted a mild form of malaria called ague.

00:18:17.442 --> 00:18:20.190
President Lincoln suffered its chills and fevers.

00:18:20.872 --> 00:18:22.297
So did Elizabeth Simcoe.

00:18:22.738 --> 00:18:25.159
wife of the first lieutenant governor of Canada.

00:18:25.979 --> 00:18:28.680
And so did the workers building the Rideau Canal.

00:18:29.740 --> 00:18:32.141
None of them knew it came from a mosquito bite.

00:18:33.821 --> 00:18:38.583
Some Anopheles could do it again today if the parasite were to come back.

00:18:40.624 --> 00:18:47.846
Chris Drakeley is the director of the Malaria Centre at the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine in England.

00:18:49.078 --> 00:18:52.721
What we have here is a female Anopheles mosquito,

00:18:53.282 --> 00:19:02.429
and this is the proboscis through which it injects its saliva into the human host when it's seeking a blood meal.

00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:05.512
When it's a malaria victim she bites,

00:19:06.112 --> 00:19:09.955
thousands of tiny parasites in the blood meal enter her body.

00:19:10.956 --> 00:19:15.640
They head straight to her stomach and rest on the outside of it,

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:17.742
taking about a week to develop.

00:19:19.002 --> 00:19:25.948
So what I'm doing here is looking at the stomach of a mosquito that fed some time ago on an infected person.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:34.016
So here are a group of parasites on the outside of the stomach there.

00:19:35.377 --> 00:19:38.540
Then the parasites move into her salivary glands.

00:19:41.462 --> 00:19:43.324
And as she bites the next person,

00:19:43.804 --> 00:19:45.506
she passes the parasites on.

00:19:47.318 --> 00:19:53.121
They grow and divide in the liver and then burst back into the bloodstream,

00:19:54.062 --> 00:19:57.304
ready to become part of another mosquito's blood meal.

00:19:59.165 --> 00:20:01.687
The person is now sick with malaria.

00:20:02.607 --> 00:20:03.988
But what about the mosquito?

00:20:06.129 --> 00:20:10.932
There are some evidence from laboratory work that suggests if you give mosquitoes a lot of malaria,

00:20:11.012 --> 00:20:12.473
feed them on a very heavy blood meal,

00:20:12.753 --> 00:20:13.954
then they do die.

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:17.592
Imagine these parasites punching holes in the mosquito's stomach.

00:20:17.632 --> 00:20:20.155
It might end up a little bit like a teabag if there's too many.

00:20:21.215 --> 00:20:24.418
The third mosquito to watch out for is the Aedes.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:30.543
These mosquitoes search out fresh puddles in a marsh,

00:20:30.583 --> 00:20:31.164
a tire,

00:20:31.444 --> 00:20:32.264
or a birdbath.

00:20:33.125 --> 00:20:37.629
Puddles can be created by snow melting over eggs laid the previous year.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:39.691
Come spring,

00:20:40.091 --> 00:20:41.712
especially in the far north,

00:20:42.073 --> 00:20:43.654
they hatch by the millions.

00:20:43.998 --> 00:20:49.683
and feast on people and any other animal passing by.

00:20:51.544 --> 00:20:54.707
A couple of Aedes mosquito species are dangerous.

00:20:55.207 --> 00:20:57.729
They spread viruses like dengue fever.

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:00.511
One is known as the Asian tiger.

00:21:01.392 --> 00:21:04.675
Hunter found a couple of them in Ontario 10 years ago.

00:21:06.516 --> 00:21:09.619
It had never been recorded in Canada before.

00:21:10.059 --> 00:21:10.560
And of course,

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:13.282
Asian tiger mosquito can transmit all sorts of...

00:21:13.834 --> 00:21:16.395
Very nasty diseases.

00:21:16.396 --> 00:21:16.635
Now,

00:21:16.995 --> 00:21:20.037
she keeps a trap at the top of a tower near the U.S.

00:21:20.117 --> 00:21:23.818
border for monitoring mosquitoes migrating into Canada.

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:29.481
When an Aedes mosquito picks up a virus,

00:21:29.881 --> 00:21:31.421
it spreads throughout her body,

00:21:32.062 --> 00:21:34.323
taking eight to ten days to develop,

00:21:34.983 --> 00:21:36.984
before ending up in her saliva,

00:21:37.824 --> 00:21:40.125
ready to be injected into a person.

00:21:41.246 --> 00:21:42.066
So you can actually...

00:21:42.414 --> 00:21:54.801
Pull off the legs of an infected mosquito and they'll have they'll have virus in them because that mosquito is so saturated in its insect blood with virus.

00:22:02.505 --> 00:22:08.269
Why do some mosquitoes pick up and transmit a virus or parasite while others don't?

00:22:09.049 --> 00:22:10.550
That's what Carl Lohenberger

00:22:10.894 --> 00:22:14.576
A parasitologist at Simon Fraser University wants to know.

00:22:16.977 --> 00:22:18.598
To keep his mosquitoes breeding,

00:22:18.858 --> 00:22:21.239
he lets them suck blood out of his own arm.

00:22:22.179 --> 00:22:24.240
Because I've done this as many times as I have,

00:22:24.941 --> 00:22:29.223
I initially feel the little pinprick as she inserts the proboscis into the skin.

00:22:29.663 --> 00:22:30.063
After that,

00:22:30.403 --> 00:22:31.304
you don't feel anything at all.

00:22:32.765 --> 00:22:34.966
He uses ice to anesthetize them.

00:22:38.282 --> 00:22:42.165
These are our experimental animals which are highly valuable.

00:22:42.965 --> 00:22:44.926
Most people don't consider mosquitoes to be valuable,

00:22:44.947 --> 00:22:47.288
but in fact we think they're tremendously exciting.

00:22:48.249 --> 00:22:49.910
These are Aedes aegypti,

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:52.432
which can transmit a virus like dengue.

00:22:53.152 --> 00:22:54.514
but never a parasite.

00:22:55.114 --> 00:22:57.117
Lohenberger wants to know why.

00:22:57.717 --> 00:23:00.361
We try to figure out when a pathogen,

00:23:00.362 --> 00:23:01.382
a parasite gets in,

00:23:02.063 --> 00:23:05.387
how does the mosquito recognize it as not being part of the mosquito?

00:23:06.808 --> 00:23:07.990
How do immune systems work?

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:09.271
How do they evolve?

00:23:09.911 --> 00:23:13.354
How does a parasite get around the immune system of the insect it's going into?

00:23:14.315 --> 00:23:15.135
To find out,

00:23:15.576 --> 00:23:18.078
he'll give this one a dose of parasites.

00:23:19.239 --> 00:23:27.305
And essentially what we do is we inject the parasites just through the neck membrane so that they can swim through the body cavity of the insect.

00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:32.931
Once the parasites are developed,

00:23:33.212 --> 00:23:34.853
the mosquitoes are dissected.

00:23:35.493 --> 00:23:39.796
It takes about a thousand of them to obtain enough body fluids to work on.

00:23:41.357 --> 00:23:48.042
In these body fluids are the proteins that the mosquito uses to reject or destroy the parasites.

00:23:48.883 --> 00:23:58.250
Lohenberger is identifying these proteins at the genetic level and creating a profile of the genes the mosquito uses to reject the parasites.

00:23:59.286 --> 00:23:59.866
In this case,

00:23:59.886 --> 00:24:02.708
what we're seeing is all these peaks represent different proteins.

00:24:02.828 --> 00:24:03.368
Ultimately,

00:24:03.769 --> 00:24:10.992
he could replicate these genes so that disease-carrying mosquitoes would reject parasites and viruses.

00:24:12.153 --> 00:24:15.395
We don't expect to be able to eliminate Aedes aegypti from the world.

00:24:15.915 --> 00:24:21.338
We would just be perfectly happy if Aedes aegypti could no longer transmit dengue virus to humans.

00:24:22.399 --> 00:24:23.199
Until then,

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:24.840
she's on the loose.

00:24:25.641 --> 00:24:27.141
And we're on the chase.

00:24:30.311 --> 00:24:35.855
And there's a state of emergency as the Fraser River overflows its banks in southern British Columbia.

00:24:35.875 --> 00:24:40.079
Blame it on climate change or increased unpredictability of weather.

00:24:40.819 --> 00:24:42.581
But in the summer of 2012,

00:24:43.001 --> 00:24:46.784
British Columbia suffered its worst flood in 40 years.

00:24:50.467 --> 00:24:51.808
Who would have thought that B.C.

00:24:52.488 --> 00:24:55.050
would also be home to a mosquito invasion?

00:24:56.535 --> 00:25:00.538
Michael Jackson is an entomologist who runs Culex Environmental,

00:25:00.938 --> 00:25:02.759
a mosquito control service.

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:05.101
When he saw the river rising,

00:25:05.501 --> 00:25:07.943
he knew immediate action was necessary,

00:25:08.503 --> 00:25:11.405
or millions of 80s mosquitoes would be unleashed.

00:25:13.146 --> 00:25:13.566
Well,

00:25:13.567 --> 00:25:18.588
I'm seeing adult mosquitoes that as the sun comes up,

00:25:19.428 --> 00:25:21.349
they tend to hide in the grass.

00:25:21.829 --> 00:25:22.490
Heavy snow,

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:23.630
then heavy rain,

00:25:24.050 --> 00:25:31.533
then hot temperatures raised the water and it washed across the shores where hundreds of thousands of mosquito eggs,

00:25:31.973 --> 00:25:33.334
many laid years ago,

00:25:33.854 --> 00:25:34.835
were lying dormant.

00:25:35.735 --> 00:25:37.337
Mosquitoes must have been thinking,

00:25:37.338 --> 00:25:37.557
you know,

00:25:37.717 --> 00:25:41.580
all that effort that I put into laying all those eggs is finally going to pay off.

00:25:43.802 --> 00:25:47.105
Some eggs are triggered to hatch on the first flood.

00:25:47.706 --> 00:25:51.169
Other eggs will wait until they're flooded twice before they hatch,

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:53.010
and others will wait three years.

00:25:53.591 --> 00:25:55.693
Where exactly were they hatching?

00:25:56.414 --> 00:26:00.437
They'd soon turn into adults if Jackson couldn't kill them first.

00:26:01.018 --> 00:26:02.439
So he mustered his troops.

00:26:04.183 --> 00:26:05.584
They found Culex larvae,

00:26:05.984 --> 00:26:08.305
potential carriers of the West Nile virus,

00:26:08.705 --> 00:26:10.025
in this murky ditch,

00:26:11.886 --> 00:26:14.587
and Aedes larvae in this flooded field.

00:26:16.128 --> 00:26:20.590
They sprayed them with a larvicide that's safe to humans and the environment.

00:26:22.671 --> 00:26:23.151
And here,

00:26:23.611 --> 00:26:26.172
where mosquitoes had bred under cottonwood trees,

00:26:26.732 --> 00:26:31.955
Jackson even employed a helicopter and hopper to target them close to the ground.

00:26:33.643 --> 00:26:36.425
We obviously try to deal with as many of the larvae as we can,

00:26:36.505 --> 00:26:38.927
but there's always going to be some that get away.

00:26:42.569 --> 00:26:42.689
So,

00:26:43.430 --> 00:26:44.110
was the B.C.

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:48.253
flood a freak occurrence or a sign of things to come?

00:26:49.154 --> 00:26:54.397
We don't know in the long term what the effects of global warming and climate change may be.

00:26:55.078 --> 00:27:00.261
Everybody is by now aware that there's something that's happening to our planet here,

00:27:00.301 --> 00:27:01.182
which is not,

00:27:01.562 --> 00:27:01.802
you know...

00:27:03.083 --> 00:27:03.263
Good.

00:27:07.387 --> 00:27:08.468
All over the world,

00:27:08.728 --> 00:27:10.890
environments are changing dramatically,

00:27:11.431 --> 00:27:13.492
and mosquitoes are taking advantage.

00:27:14.193 --> 00:27:15.194
In the worst cases,

00:27:15.534 --> 00:27:18.917
a couple of Aedes species are spreading dengue fever.

00:27:20.238 --> 00:27:22.541
Also known as breakbone disease,

00:27:23.021 --> 00:27:24.682
dengue can be deadly.

00:27:25.683 --> 00:27:28.786
Half the world's population is now at risk of it.

00:27:31.775 --> 00:27:37.377
Robin Lindsay is Chief of Field Studies at the National Biological Laboratory in Winnipeg.

00:27:38.158 --> 00:27:43.740
Lindsay is on Canada's front line when it comes to tracking potentially dangerous mosquitoes.

00:27:45.501 --> 00:27:47.361
They are what I like to call hardwired.

00:27:47.461 --> 00:27:49.402
They do things on an instinctual basis.

00:27:49.422 --> 00:27:51.023
They don't think about what they're doing.

00:27:51.063 --> 00:27:52.404
They just do things.

00:27:52.544 --> 00:27:59.727
So they are certainly able to take advantage of opportunities as they present themselves.

00:28:03.499 --> 00:28:04.439
This is Dhaka,

00:28:04.919 --> 00:28:06.520
the capital of Bangladesh,

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:09.001
and a hotspot for dengue fever.

00:28:10.821 --> 00:28:10.941
For

00:28:11.341 --> 00:28:12.201
80s mosquitoes,

00:28:12.722 --> 00:28:14.342
Dhaka is paradise.

00:28:16.823 --> 00:28:20.544
What we're seeing here is this obviously tropical vegetation.

00:28:20.564 --> 00:28:22.524
You can't feel the heat here,

00:28:22.544 --> 00:28:25.705
but this area is very tropical based on the heat and humidity,

00:28:25.706 --> 00:28:28.366
and this creates a perfect environment for mosquitoes.

00:28:28.726 --> 00:28:29.566
Hosts in the front,

00:28:30.626 --> 00:28:31.867
living quarters in the back.

00:28:33.111 --> 00:28:37.934
Hades mosquitoes love the humidity but need fresh still water for breeding.

00:28:38.574 --> 00:28:39.835
So where are they hiding?

00:28:40.635 --> 00:28:42.036
That's why Lindsay is here.

00:28:42.957 --> 00:28:45.518
There's no way that that...

00:28:45.618 --> 00:28:46.419
This is fascinating.

00:28:46.639 --> 00:28:48.940
I keep a colony of these mosquitoes back in Winnipeg,

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.321
but to see them in the flesh,

00:28:51.381 --> 00:28:51.982
so to speak,

00:28:52.282 --> 00:28:53.002
is quite amazing.

00:28:53.022 --> 00:28:57.525
They are almost like the field mouse of the mosquito world.

00:28:57.565 --> 00:29:00.947
They're completely adapted to living in human habitation.

00:29:02.287 --> 00:29:04.068
Some of them are coiled.

00:29:04.069 --> 00:29:08.891
Lindsay is kick-starting a surveillance program at Dhaka's North-South University.

00:29:09.992 --> 00:29:14.674
I know many of the research fellows have looked over the sheets and have some questions.

00:29:14.675 --> 00:29:19.517
He's plotted out the city on a grid and assigned teams to cover each area.

00:29:20.077 --> 00:29:21.098
In his own container...

00:29:21.918 --> 00:29:25.000
Lindsay was brought to Bangladesh by Emdad Haque.

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:26.461
So what we need to do,

00:29:26.541 --> 00:29:30.103
we need to throw the dice and get that number and then...

00:29:30.143 --> 00:29:33.245
Walk to that house.

00:29:33.246 --> 00:29:35.766
Haque is a professor at the University of Manitoba,

00:29:36.286 --> 00:29:38.468
but he was born and raised in Bangladesh,

00:29:38.868 --> 00:29:41.149
and this project is his brainchild.

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:49.134
It's a four-year study of how social and environmental changes affect the spread of dengue.

00:29:51.135 --> 00:29:54.617
This project could be a milestone for the entire region,

00:29:54.997 --> 00:29:58.079
so we are hoping that we can create a new frontier.

00:29:59.083 --> 00:30:02.345
and work together with the community here in the Asian regions.

00:30:05.746 --> 00:30:08.507
Dhaka is the world's newest megacity.

00:30:11.349 --> 00:30:14.130
Millions of migrants from the countryside are moving in,

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:17.332
pushing the population toward 20 million.

00:30:20.973 --> 00:30:24.635
It's the thriving textile and garment industry they've come for.

00:30:31.383 --> 00:30:33.304
But the city can't keep up with them.

00:30:36.125 --> 00:30:38.866
There's an explosion of slums and high-rises,

00:30:42.748 --> 00:30:45.229
and mosquitoes are making the most of it.

00:30:46.869 --> 00:30:48.370
Mosquitoes are part of life here,

00:30:49.250 --> 00:30:51.271
but the frontier with mosquitoes got changed.

00:30:51.771 --> 00:30:52.832
It used to be bushy,

00:30:52.992 --> 00:30:53.512
wet land.

00:30:53.532 --> 00:30:54.412
You can see around,

00:30:54.432 --> 00:30:54.632
I mean,

00:30:54.692 --> 00:30:55.873
still just behind this,

00:30:56.213 --> 00:30:57.233
those are wet lands.

00:30:57.694 --> 00:31:00.795
And you can see that it used to be wet land.

00:31:00.975 --> 00:31:02.475
flora and fauna.

00:31:03.936 --> 00:31:05.536
There were always mosquitoes here,

00:31:06.156 --> 00:31:10.518
but the new buildings created a habitat for dengue carriers.

00:31:13.678 --> 00:31:14.999
When you go and survey these houses,

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:16.459
it's very hard to find the mosquitoes.

00:31:16.479 --> 00:31:17.760
They're there and they're biting people,

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:20.560
but the abundance is quite low.

00:31:21.001 --> 00:31:26.462
The best way to count them is to add up the larvae and pupae breeding in water containers.

00:31:28.222 --> 00:31:29.843
This house is mosquito-free.

00:31:32.235 --> 00:31:33.276
But outside it,

00:31:33.656 --> 00:31:35.056
the team finds larvae.

00:31:38.757 --> 00:31:41.258
And then they find some at a construction site.

00:31:41.958 --> 00:31:43.098
This is an eye-opener.

00:31:43.739 --> 00:31:46.799
Puddles are ignored in places like this by everyone,

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:49.680
except the dengue-bearing mosquito.

00:31:52.801 --> 00:31:53.721
In Bangladesh,

00:31:54.121 --> 00:31:57.002
dengue fever used to be called Dhaka fever.

00:31:57.782 --> 00:31:59.603
Dhaka Medical College Hospital

00:31:59.987 --> 00:32:03.408
admits six to eight dengue fever patients daily.

00:32:05.248 --> 00:32:06.449
What is the problem here?

00:32:06.469 --> 00:32:09.330
There are four people.

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:11.750
This has come from mosquito bites,

00:32:11.751 --> 00:32:12.430
no doubt about that.

00:32:12.431 --> 00:32:15.551
What other problems were there with the water?

00:32:15.552 --> 00:32:17.472
In 2000 there was a major outbreak.

00:32:17.932 --> 00:32:18.812
It was an epidemic.

00:32:19.192 --> 00:32:22.713
More than officially 5,000 people got hospitalized,

00:32:22.714 --> 00:32:25.374
but our prediction it could be more than 10,000.

00:32:26.414 --> 00:32:28.795
Today the team has come to a slum.

00:32:29.591 --> 00:32:30.111
In Dhaka,

00:32:30.371 --> 00:32:33.612
dengue is perceived as a disease of the affluent.

00:32:34.292 --> 00:32:36.693
That's because the rich get hospital treatment,

00:32:37.113 --> 00:32:38.874
and their cases are recorded.

00:32:39.574 --> 00:32:40.654
The poor get sick,

00:32:41.214 --> 00:32:42.134
below the radar.

00:32:43.455 --> 00:32:44.635
But the Aedes mosquito,

00:32:44.755 --> 00:32:45.315
it seems,

00:32:45.575 --> 00:32:46.876
does not discriminate.

00:32:47.556 --> 00:32:48.936
There's standing water here,

00:32:49.036 --> 00:32:49.256
too,

00:32:49.877 --> 00:32:51.417
and larvae are breeding in it.

00:32:53.398 --> 00:32:54.038
In the end,

00:32:54.598 --> 00:32:58.559
larvae mostly showed up where this stressed city's water systems...

00:32:58.739 --> 00:33:00.439
are inadequate.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:01.540
This is a Jap Thai.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:02.840
They say that there are some.

00:33:03.880 --> 00:33:04.741
It takes some samples.

00:33:05.761 --> 00:33:05.881
So,

00:33:06.261 --> 00:33:06.821
in Dhaka,

00:33:07.501 --> 00:33:10.562
are disease-bearing mosquitoes the price of progress?

00:33:10.982 --> 00:33:11.382
Worse,

00:33:11.863 --> 00:33:13.123
are they winning the battle?

00:33:14.123 --> 00:33:15.624
I don't even think it's a draw right now.

00:33:15.944 --> 00:33:16.324
They are,

00:33:16.964 --> 00:33:19.645
in places like this and even in North America,

00:33:20.205 --> 00:33:21.165
in many ways are winning.

00:33:23.446 --> 00:33:23.806
For M.

00:33:23.826 --> 00:33:24.446
Dadhak,

00:33:24.926 --> 00:33:27.867
trying to stay on top of the dengue-bearing mosquito you

00:33:28.235 --> 00:33:29.576
is an endless task.

00:33:30.577 --> 00:33:36.221
First you are trying to understand what is the transmission process and then we'll try with some intervention.

00:33:36.621 --> 00:33:40.704
So from there we learn and again we'll come back and we'll sort of continue to work.

00:33:41.064 --> 00:33:42.285
So it's an impossible task.

00:33:42.625 --> 00:33:44.087
I do agree but we cannot give up.

00:33:46.168 --> 00:33:50.291
What's happening in Dhaka repeats itself across Southeast Asia.

00:33:51.112 --> 00:33:55.655
Mosquitoes are thriving hand in hand with rapid urban expansion.

00:33:56.951 --> 00:34:00.194
They're also flourishing because of globalization.

00:34:00.774 --> 00:34:02.556
With so much international travel,

00:34:02.936 --> 00:34:04.597
mosquitoes can hitch a ride,

00:34:04.978 --> 00:34:05.538
settle in,

00:34:06.058 --> 00:34:07.259
and spread diseases.

00:34:09.641 --> 00:34:10.262
Back home,

00:34:10.742 --> 00:34:14.765
Robin Lindsay keeps his eyes peeled for dengue-bearing mosquitoes,

00:34:15.286 --> 00:34:17.288
particularly the Asian tiger,

00:34:17.648 --> 00:34:18.829
which is creeping north,

00:34:19.249 --> 00:34:20.730
up through the United States,

00:34:21.151 --> 00:34:21.891
toward Canada.

00:34:22.952 --> 00:34:26.195
If climate change continues to expand the...

00:34:26.635 --> 00:34:30.123
range the potential range of that mosquito is certainly theoretically possible

00:34:30.663 --> 00:34:33.746
that we could have dengue cases occurring here.

00:34:35.507 --> 00:34:38.210
Our troubles with mosquitoes are getting worse.

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:40.712
There must be something we can do,

00:34:41.592 --> 00:34:42.073
isn't there?

00:34:45.315 --> 00:34:46.496
They zap us.

00:34:47.157 --> 00:34:48.618
How do we zap them?

00:34:49.619 --> 00:34:52.061
Scientists have come up with hundreds of ideas,

00:34:52.581 --> 00:34:56.004
like this laser zapper that's triggered by wingbeats,

00:34:58.286 --> 00:34:59.667
or this pesticide-laden

00:34:59.807 --> 00:35:01.988
paint that kills them on contact.

00:35:03.729 --> 00:35:06.991
Predators are being introduced to devour larvae.

00:35:07.791 --> 00:35:12.514
And insect-eating plants are being grown in malaria-infested areas.

00:35:13.815 --> 00:35:19.017
Fiona Hunter and Ainsley Teelman are testing a garlic-based mosquito repellent,

00:35:19.558 --> 00:35:19.878
but...

00:35:22.059 --> 00:35:24.120
Obviously with this many mosquitoes in the trap,

00:35:24.540 --> 00:35:26.461
the garlic isn't having a huge effect.

00:35:27.362 --> 00:35:29.103
Finding something that works.

00:35:29.483 --> 00:35:30.444
is never ending.

00:35:31.184 --> 00:35:36.468
I think if we look at all the efforts that we're making to try and control mosquitoes,

00:35:37.669 --> 00:35:40.471
they just keep one step ahead of us,

00:35:40.891 --> 00:35:41.311
I think,

00:35:41.871 --> 00:35:44.573
that every time we come up with something,

00:35:44.653 --> 00:35:49.216
they evolve a counter-strategy to outwit us.

00:35:52.158 --> 00:35:52.459
Once,

00:35:52.539 --> 00:35:54.260
we thought we had a perfect tool.

00:35:55.040 --> 00:35:55.261
In the

00:35:56.041 --> 00:35:56.862
1940s and 50s,

00:35:57.162 --> 00:35:58.363
we sprayed DDT

00:35:58.991 --> 00:36:01.172
to fight malaria in parts of Asia,

00:36:01.752 --> 00:36:03.053
North and South America,

00:36:03.773 --> 00:36:04.193
Africa,

00:36:04.673 --> 00:36:06.094
Australia and Europe.

00:36:07.895 --> 00:36:09.275
In Sardinia alone,

00:36:09.695 --> 00:36:14.477
about 250 tons of it were sprayed on every mosquito habitat.

00:36:15.278 --> 00:36:17.919
Five years and seven million dollars later,

00:36:18.459 --> 00:36:19.660
malaria was gone.

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:27.783
DDT was the super insecticide until...

00:36:28.743 --> 00:36:34.585
The public was being asked to accept these chemicals,

00:36:34.586 --> 00:36:35.365
was being asked to acquiesce in their use.

00:36:35.366 --> 00:36:37.205
Rachel Carson wrote Silent Spring,

00:36:37.826 --> 00:36:40.866
exposing its repercussions through the food chain.

00:36:43.387 --> 00:36:43.607
In the

00:36:44.588 --> 00:36:48.169
1970s, DDT was banned in Canada and the U.S.,

00:36:48.569 --> 00:36:50.749
and eventually in most of the world,

00:36:51.349 --> 00:36:52.870
even for fighting malaria.

00:36:55.151 --> 00:36:56.491
Nothing could replace it.

00:36:57.047 --> 00:37:00.329
And the ban was recently lifted in high-risk areas.

00:37:01.169 --> 00:37:02.030
For mosquitoes,

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:03.631
the ban didn't matter.

00:37:04.231 --> 00:37:06.552
They were already ahead of the game.

00:37:07.893 --> 00:37:11.035
Mosquitoes can develop a resistance to insecticides.

00:37:12.155 --> 00:37:16.098
Their exoskeletons can absorb them and form a protective wall.

00:37:17.918 --> 00:37:21.901
Or a nerve the insecticides are targeting can be desensitized.

00:37:22.681 --> 00:37:24.722
When the changes are genetically based,

00:37:25.102 --> 00:37:25.963
the resistance...

00:37:26.203 --> 00:37:27.984
is passed on to her offspring.

00:37:28.905 --> 00:37:29.865
It is a huge issue.

00:37:30.545 --> 00:37:31.786
You can't stop evolution.

00:37:32.246 --> 00:37:37.729
You will get evolution of strains that are out there that are resistant to chemicals.

00:37:38.250 --> 00:37:43.313
Steve Lindsay is a public health entomologist at Durham University in England.

00:37:43.773 --> 00:37:47.095
If you want to develop control against this mosquito,

00:37:47.155 --> 00:37:51.917
you have to get yourself into the head of the mosquito and think like a mosquito.

00:37:53.738 --> 00:37:54.679
Each species...

00:37:54.999 --> 00:37:57.520
is a specialist with its own feeding strategy.

00:37:58.240 --> 00:37:59.861
So if it's malaria-bearing,

00:38:00.241 --> 00:38:00.701
indoor,

00:38:00.842 --> 00:38:03.663
night-feeding Anopheles mosquitoes you're up against,

00:38:04.203 --> 00:38:05.644
bed nets are the answer.

00:38:07.885 --> 00:38:09.985
Bed nets have saved millions of lives.

00:38:10.646 --> 00:38:12.967
What's next are window and door screens.

00:38:13.727 --> 00:38:15.928
Lindsay is introducing them in West Africa,

00:38:16.368 --> 00:38:19.550
and already they've cut the number of bites in half.

00:38:21.770 --> 00:38:22.831
But screened out...

00:38:23.319 --> 00:38:24.960
These mosquitoes have adapted.

00:38:25.661 --> 00:38:25.961
Now,

00:38:26.501 --> 00:38:27.842
they're outdoor feeders.

00:38:29.643 --> 00:38:31.304
So we're left scratching our head thinking,

00:38:31.465 --> 00:38:32.005
what can we do?

00:38:33.426 --> 00:38:34.086
For years,

00:38:34.627 --> 00:38:35.187
Winnipeg,

00:38:35.447 --> 00:38:37.208
the mosquito capital of Canada,

00:38:37.669 --> 00:38:38.669
has had the answer.

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:39.910
Spray them.

00:38:43.173 --> 00:38:44.493
They're a great foal to fight every day.

00:38:44.934 --> 00:38:48.036
Taz Stewart is city entomologist for Winnipeg.

00:38:51.858 --> 00:38:52.479
Every summer,

00:38:52.875 --> 00:38:59.318
Stuart orders his ground and helicopter crews to kill larvae with a variety of bacterial insecticides,

00:39:00.118 --> 00:39:01.959
including one known as BTI.

00:39:02.299 --> 00:39:02.939
It's right there.

00:39:03.039 --> 00:39:03.920
It's a little corn cob.

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:06.401
The BTI is a dead bacteria on there.

00:39:06.402 --> 00:39:07.441
It gives them a bad stomach ache.

00:39:07.521 --> 00:39:07.881
They die.

00:39:08.742 --> 00:39:11.003
It's particularly effective against larvae.

00:39:12.483 --> 00:39:17.285
Stuart is willing to use a chemical insecticide to fog adult mosquitoes,

00:39:17.826 --> 00:39:19.126
but it's a last resort.

00:39:23.235 --> 00:39:27.654
There is something that could stop dangerous mosquitoes in their tracks.

00:39:30.223 --> 00:39:34.584
Anthony James is a molecular biologist at the University of California,

00:39:34.784 --> 00:39:35.244
Irvine.

00:39:35.885 --> 00:39:38.305
Genetic engineering is his specialty.

00:39:40.746 --> 00:39:45.927
James creates mutant larvae that carry a gene resistant to the dengue virus.

00:39:46.628 --> 00:39:50.188
Green fluorescence is a marker that tells him it's present.

00:39:51.209 --> 00:39:51.509
This is,

00:39:51.609 --> 00:39:51.949
once again,

00:39:52.029 --> 00:39:55.530
something you can't see because it's an atomic level almost in a sense.

00:39:55.531 --> 00:39:55.650
I mean,

00:39:55.651 --> 00:39:56.870
we're looking at molecules,

00:39:56.890 --> 00:39:59.171
and so we can build something in...

00:39:59.631 --> 00:40:02.972
A test tube using modern molecular biology techniques.

00:40:03.472 --> 00:40:03.992
For that,

00:40:04.332 --> 00:40:05.273
they need some eggs,

00:40:05.553 --> 00:40:08.333
which these mosquitoes will lay in a test tube.

00:40:11.874 --> 00:40:13.915
Only the most robust are selected.

00:40:15.095 --> 00:40:15.275
Then,

00:40:15.335 --> 00:40:17.996
with a drop of DNA on the end of a needle,

00:40:18.536 --> 00:40:20.697
the gene is injected into the eggs.

00:40:24.298 --> 00:40:25.338
In a couple of days,

00:40:25.678 --> 00:40:27.879
genetically modified larvae emerge.

00:40:29.283 --> 00:40:31.424
So when we look at this and we see the green eyes,

00:40:31.464 --> 00:40:35.366
we know that this particular mosquito is carrying the gene that we want in it.

00:40:35.966 --> 00:40:37.606
So it's been transformed.

00:40:37.847 --> 00:40:41.368
Because a normal mosquito would not have green eyes that would fluoresce like this.

00:40:42.569 --> 00:40:43.429
Not only that,

00:40:43.789 --> 00:40:47.831
but future generations will inherit the new dengue-resistant gene.

00:40:50.072 --> 00:40:52.493
James is also working on one for malaria.

00:40:54.153 --> 00:40:57.775
He uses the same technique to create a flightless mosquito.

00:40:58.571 --> 00:40:59.391
It can't fly,

00:40:59.491 --> 00:41:00.992
so it can't mate and breed,

00:41:01.632 --> 00:41:03.413
so it can't transmit a disease.

00:41:05.054 --> 00:41:08.395
Could genetic modification be our ultimate weapon?

00:41:08.855 --> 00:41:10.836
Or could there be unintended,

00:41:10.996 --> 00:41:17.779
devastating consequences if millions of genetically engineered mosquitoes were released into the environment?

00:41:18.519 --> 00:41:19.880
Is it worth the risk?

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:26.603
We think that within the discipline and being very careful and taking phased approaches and having...

00:41:27.183 --> 00:41:30.085
The ability to respond to problems that

00:41:30.985 --> 00:41:33.207
I think there's a reason to go ahead and try this.

00:41:33.927 --> 00:41:36.428
The alternative is to give in to the disease,

00:41:36.468 --> 00:41:41.852
which is something that is almost as a species we've decided we're not going to do.

00:41:42.952 --> 00:41:44.533
If we're going to win the battle,

00:41:45.073 --> 00:41:47.735
there are many mysteries still to be solved.

00:41:48.335 --> 00:41:49.276
For Fiona Hunter,

00:41:49.656 --> 00:41:51.337
that has become her life work.

00:41:52.678 --> 00:41:54.419
It fills me with awe.

00:41:54.779 --> 00:42:00.801
and wonder at how that female mosquito can survive in a place like this and be so successful.

00:42:03.863 --> 00:42:04.483
In the end,

00:42:04.863 --> 00:42:06.704
she's a creature of contradictions.

00:42:07.244 --> 00:42:09.085
She simply wants to reproduce,

00:42:09.465 --> 00:42:11.146
but that's what makes her dangerous.

00:42:13.207 --> 00:42:15.267
Her delicate wings are a marvel,

00:42:15.948 --> 00:42:17.969
but their buzzing sound torments us.

00:42:19.029 --> 00:42:21.170
Her eyes are stunningly beautiful,

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:23.891
but they help her find our flesh.

00:42:25.199 --> 00:42:29.320
Her proboscis is a perfect model for painless hypodermic needles.

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:32.921
But what it deposits in our veins could kill us.

00:42:34.041 --> 00:42:36.462
Her survival instincts are astounding.

00:42:37.002 --> 00:42:39.723
But that just means there are billions more of her.

00:42:42.304 --> 00:42:43.284
One thing is certain.

00:42:44.064 --> 00:42:44.744
This tiny,

00:42:44.884 --> 00:42:47.645
fragile creature holds power over us.

00:42:48.285 --> 00:42:48.625
And so,

00:42:49.486 --> 00:42:50.886
our battle continues.

00:42:52.226 --> 00:42:54.367
She was here long before us.

00:42:55.308 --> 00:42:56.906
She may outlive us.

